<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">standing</ansi>

Settlements judge your company by how close your alignment
(<ansi fg="command">help alignment</ansi>) is to their own. That standing
decides what you pay at market and at the inn, and whether they'll deal
with you at all.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">How standing is judged</ansi>

Your company's standing in a settlement comes from the gap between your
company's average alignment and the settlement's own:

  <ansi fg="yellow">welcome</ansi>      a small gap: normal prices everywhere
  <ansi fg="yellow">tolerated</ansi>    a larger gap: still normal prices
  <ansi fg="yellow">distrusted</ansi>   a bigger gap still: markets and inns charge more
  <ansi fg="yellow">shunned</ansi>      too far apart: markets and inns turn you away

Being distrusted or shunned only ever costs you; a company in especially
good standing pays no less than the normal price.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Effects</ansi>

A distrusted company pays more to buy at any market here, is paid less
when selling, and pays more for a room at any inn here. A shunned company
is refused by every ordinary market and inn in the settlement.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Black markets</ansi>

Some settlements hide a black market: an out-of-the-way spot that trades
at ordinary prices, but only with companies the settlement distrusts or
shuns. A company in good standing gets turned away there instead - a
black market has no reason to deal with someone the town already
welcomes.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Usage:</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">standing</ansi>
  Shows your company's standing here (both alignments, the tier, and its
  effects), if this is a settlement, then every other settlement's tier,
  so you can plan ahead.

See also: <ansi fg="command">help alignment</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help company</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help market</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help inn</ansi>.